ops/codex/build-sandbox-limits.log:14:Read AGENTS.md (your role: implementer) and CLAUDE.md. Implement: Dec 020 item 6 + dec 021 item 4 (read ops/decisions/020-*.md and 021-*.md). Harden the LOCAL subprocess sandbox executor in eval/sandbox_env.py (the execute() wrapper installed by the hook that replaces swegemma.sandbox.subprocess.execute_subprocess_command; the original is adk_eval_core/sandbox/subprocess_sandbox.py:execute_subprocess_command in /home/ubuntu/gemma-local/venv/lib/python3.13/site-packages, read it to mirror its return type and semantics exactly). Context: a local gold/null run OOM-stalled this shared 12 GB VM; two null-patch tasks ran ~45 min each despite 300 s command timeouts, with no per-sandbox memory limit and no RSS logging. Requirements: (1) New module eval/proctree.py (stdlib only, Linux /proc): snapshot of our-uid processes (pid, ppid, pgid, sid, rss, starttime), descendants(), tree_rss(), kill_tree(pids, verify starttime to avoid pid reuse), survivors(). (2) Our executor runs the command itself (do not call the original), with Popen(start_new_session=True), stdout/stderr captured exactly like the original (same decoding/truncation behaviour, same result object type and fields, same status values incl. 'timeout' and exit_code 124 on timeout), polling every 0.25 s: track the set of every pid ever seen in the tree (descendants of proc.pid, plus anything in its pgid/sid) so setsid'd or reparented orphans are still tracked; sum RSS over live tracked pids; record peak. (3) Memory limit: if summed tree RSS > GEMMA_SANDBOX_MEM_BYTES (default 4 GiB, the scorer sandbox limit) SIGKILL all tracked pids, return exit_code 137 with status 'error' (or the closest existing non-success status the original uses) and a clear stderr note 'killed: sandbox memory limit'. Backstop: prefix the command with prlimit --as=<GEMMA_SANDBOX_AS_BYTES, default 8 GiB> -- (skip if prlimit missing). (4) On timeout: SIGKILL every tracked live pid (killpg plus individual kills), then assert no survivors (re-scan up to 2 s); on any normal exit also kill any tracked survivors (background daemons left by tests) and count them. (5) Metrics: in the existing command_metrics ContextVar dict record peak_rss_bytes (max over commands), mem_kills, orphan_kills; and have eval/kaggle/gold_calibration.py write peak_rss_bytes, mem_kills, orphan_kills into each result row (numbers only, never task text) and log one line per task. Also add env GOLD_CONCURRENCY (default keep current 2) so a local run can use 1. (6) Must keep working with bwrap (--unshare-pid: inner processes are visible in host /proc with host pids) and without bwrap (hidden_paths empty). Tests: new eval/tests/test_sandbox_limits.py: (a) timeout with an orphaning grandchild via bash '(sleep 600 &); sleep 600' and a setsid'd grandchild via python os.setsid -> status timeout, exit 124, zero survivors; (b) memory: a child allocating ~600 MB in touched steps with GEMMA_SANDBOX_MEM_BYTES=300MB -> killed, 137, mem_kills=1; (c) normal command output/exit code identical to the original executor's for a few commands (echo, exit 3, stderr), peak_rss_bytes>0; (d) background daemon left after normal exit is killed and counted. Keep every test allocation <= 1 GB and test wall time < 60 s. Run ONLY eval/tests/test_sandbox_limits.py and eval/tests/test_eval_fixes.py plus eval/tests/test_gold_calibration.py (python: /home/ubuntu/gemma-local/venv/bin/python3 with /home/ubuntu/gemma-local/pyimg/cpython-3.13.18-linux-x86_64-gnu/lib/python3.13/site-packages appended to sys.path, then pytest.main); do not run the full suite or any real gold calibration. No new dependencies.
